.TH CANOPA-NOTIFY 1 "November 2026" "Canopa Notify" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
canopa-notify \- Wayland desktop notifications and local notification history
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B canopa-notify daemon --service
.RB [ --socket " PATH"]
.RB [ --database " PATH"]
.RB [ --config " PATH" | --canopa-config " PATH"]
.br
.B canopa-notify daemon --headless
.RB [ --socket " PATH"]
.RB [ --database " PATH"]
.RB [ --config " PATH" | --canopa-config " PATH"]
.RB [ --dev-bus-name " NAME"]
.br
.B canopa-notify ctl
.RB [ --socket " PATH"]
.I COMMAND
.br
.B canopa-notify panel-provider
.RB [ --socket " PATH"]
.RB [ --protocol " canopa-v5|canopa-v4"]
.br
.B canopa-notify check-config
.RB [ --config " PATH" | --canopa-config " PATH" | --defaults ]
.RB [ --show-source ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Canopa Notify implements the freedesktop Desktop Notifications service. In service
mode it owns org.freedesktop.Notifications and
org.freedesktop.impl.portal.desktop.canopa.notify (the Notification portal backend,
version 2) on the session bus, shows banners that never take keyboard focus, and
serves the Notification Center hosted by Canopa Shell. Service mode requires a
Wayland session with the wlr layer-shell protocol.
.PP
Headless mode shows nothing and is meant for tests and development. It does not
own the freedesktop name; it joins the session bus only under a name requested
with
.BR --dev-bus-name .
.PP
Startup fails if another process already owns the notification service name or
the control socket. Restart invalidates existing sender IDs and actions. Retained
notifications keep their unread state; transient notifications do not survive a
restart.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B daemon
Run the notification service. Exactly one of
.B --service
or
.B --headless
is required.
.B --config
selects a legacy-format file and
.B --canopa-config
a shared Canopa file; without either, the configuration is discovered.
.TP
.B ctl
Send one command to the running daemon. It never starts the daemon.
.TP
.B panel-provider
Serve the Notification Center to a panel host over stdin and stdout. The default
protocol is canopa-v5, which Canopa Shell's built-in Center uses; canopa-v4
remains for existing hosts and cannot carry activation tokens.
.TP
.B check-config
Validate the notifications namespace without starting a service and print one JSON
line. --show-source prints local diagnostic detail on stderr. The
.B canopa config check
command checks every Canopa component together.
.SH CONTROL COMMANDS
.TP
.B status [--json]
Report daemon, configuration, rendering, sender-publication and link-opening health.
.TP
.B active [--json]
Count, or list, active retained notifications.
.TP
.B dnd [status|on|off|toggle|tomorrow]
Read or change Do Not Disturb. The default is status. tomorrow turns it on until
08:00 the next morning.
.TP
.B dnd on --for MINUTES
.TQ
.B dnd on --until HH:MM
Turn Do Not Disturb on until a time: up to seven days from now, or the next
occurrence of HH:MM in local time.
.TP
.B dnd-schedule FROM TO [--days DAYS] [--alarms] [--calls]
Set the daily Do Not Disturb schedule from FROM to TO (HH:MM). DAYS is a quoted
list such as "mon tue wed"; the default is every day. --alarms lets alarm.*
notifications and --calls lets call.incoming notifications break through.
.TP
.B dnd-schedule --clear
Remove the schedule and break-through set through the control interface, returning
to the configuration file's values.
.TP
.B preferences
Print Do Not Disturb and per-application preferences as JSON.
.TP
.B app APP_ID [--delivery allow|silence|drop] [--sound true|false] [--lock-screen hidden|name-only|content]
Set one application's delivery (show banners, save quietly, or block), sound and
lock-screen visibility. Omitted options are cleared.
.TP
.B read-all
Mark every notification read.
.TP
.B clear-all
Dismiss, mark read and hide every notification from the Center. Prints an undo
token.
.TP
.B undo-clear TOKEN
Undo the latest clear-all within 30 seconds.
.TP
.B clear
Dismiss active notifications, including transient ones.
.TP
.B delete-history
Remove inactive retained history, including unread items.
.TP
.B reload [PATH]
Validate and install a configuration. Without PATH, repeat the current selection,
discovering the configuration again when the daemon started without an explicit
file. PATH selects a legacy-format file. A failed reload keeps the previous
configuration.
.TP
.B reload-canopa PATH
Install the notifications namespace of an explicit shared Canopa file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopa/config.kdl
Shared Canopa configuration; Notify reads its notifications block. The fallback
directory is ~/.config. A missing block uses the embedded defaults.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopa-notify/config.kdl
Legacy configuration, used only when the shared file is absent.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/canopa-notify/history.db
History, unread state, Do Not Disturb state, timers and schedule, and
per-application preferences. The fallback is ~/.local/state/canopa-notify/history.db.
Back it up while the daemon is stopped.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/canopa-notify/control.sock
Default control socket for daemon, ctl and panel-provider when --socket is omitted.
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR must be an absolute path.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/canopa-notify.service
User unit installed by the Arch package; it runs /usr/bin/canopa-notify and is
started by canopa-session.target in the Canopa session. Local installations use
the same unit in ~/.config/systemd/user with ~/.local/bin/canopa-notify.
.SH OPERATION
Stop any other notification daemon before starting the service. The daemon does
not watch its configuration file: run
.B canopa-notify ctl reload
after editing it. Consult
.B journalctl --user -u canopa-notify.service
for diagnostics.
.PP
Database migrations are forward only. Restore a backup taken before the upgrade
before running an older binary. If status reports that the banner renderer
stopped, notifications are still received and the Center still works; restart the
service to restore banners.
.PP
Body links accept HTTP and HTTPS and open through the desktop OpenURI portal.
Opening a link does not invoke the sender's default action or dismiss the
notification.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Nonzero indicates invalid arguments, startup failure,
rejected control input or an unsuccessful exchange. A failed exchange after
transmission can have an unknown outcome; inspect the state before retrying.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR notify-send (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
